---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
const desc = 'Why Tek Media Group replaces the capacitors that need it instead of recapping every board: risks, benefits and our approach by equipment type.';
const boardRisk = [
  ['1960s–70s phenolic', 'Extreme', 'Lifted traces, loose pads, delamination'],
  ['1970s–80s single-sided FR4', 'High', 'Broken plated holes, flex-cracked joints'],
  ['1980s–90s double-sided FR4', 'Moderate', 'Heat damage to nearby semiconductors'],
  ['1990s+ lead-free', 'Moderate', 'Higher heat needed, more via stress'],
  ['Multi-layer (any era)', 'Very high', 'Internal plated-hole damage, often unrepairable'],
];
const approach = [
  ['Vintage tube gear (high voltage)', 'Replace all electrolytic and paper capacitors for safety'],
  ['Solid-state gear over 30 years old', 'Replace main power-supply capacitors; signal path only if sound is degraded'],
  ['Working, good-sounding units', 'Replace only failed or suspicious parts'],
  ['High-value collectibles (Marantz, McIntosh, B&O)', 'Partial recap with high-grade audio parts'],
  ['Budget or daily-driver units', 'Replace only visibly leaking or bulging parts'],
];
---
<Base title="Capacitor Replacement: Pros and Cons" description={desc}>
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="wrap">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / Capacitor replacement</p>
    <h1>Capacitor replacement: the logic behind our approach</h1>
    <p>Why we replace the capacitors that need it, instead of every capacitor on the board.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap prose">
    <p>
      After three decades at the bench, we've seen what full "recaps" do to vintage equipment, good and bad. This page
      explains how we decide which capacitors to replace. It's an account of our experience, not a how-to guide.
    </p>

    <h2>Targeted replacement vs. full recap</h2>
    <p>We replace capacitors that are failing, out of spec or suspicious, and leave healthy parts alone.</p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li><strong>Lower cost.</strong> A full recap can cost two to three times more in parts and labor.</li>
      <li><strong>Less board stress.</strong> Every joint we heat is a chance to lift a trace or damage a via.</li>
      <li><strong>Original sound preserved.</strong> Swapping every part can change the character of vintage gear.</li>
      <li><strong>Reliability where it matters.</strong> Modern 105°C low-ESR parts go in critical power-supply positions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why "replace them all" isn't always better</h2>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li><strong>The domino effect is mostly a myth.</strong> A capacitor usually fails from local heat or a bad batch, not because its neighbors are failing.</li>
      <li><strong>Thermal stress.</strong> Heating each pad risks lifted traces, damaged vias and delamination.</li>
      <li><strong>The bathtub curve.</strong> Parts that have survived for years are stable. New parts bring back early-life failure risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Impedance matters.</strong> In switch-mode supplies, generic replacements can cause instability, ripple or noise.</li>
      <li><strong>Old stock.</strong> Capacitors stored for years may need reforming before use.</li>
      <li><strong>Film, ceramic and mica capacitors</strong> rarely fail, so replacing them adds risk for little gain.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Our approach by equipment type</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Equipment</th><th>What we do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{approach.map((r) => <tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td>{r[1]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h2>Board risk by era</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Board type</th><th>Risk</th><th>Main danger</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{boardRisk.map((r) => <tr><td>{r[0]}</td><td>{r[1]}</td><td>{r[2]}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h2>Risks we see from recapping</h2>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>Lifted or broken traces, especially on 1960s–70s phenolic boards</li>
      <li>Damaged plated-through holes causing intermittent faults</li>
      <li>Heat damage to nearby transistors and diodes that shows up days later</li>
      <li>Brittle wiring and snapped connector tabs</li>
      <li>Static damage to sensitive chips in tuner and remote circuits</li>
      <li>A brighter, harsher sound from low-ESR parts in the signal path</li>
      <li>Hidden faults unmasked, such as oscillation or noisy transistors</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Beware of cheap parts and kits</h2>
    <p>
      We regularly see counterfeit or relabeled capacitors, degraded "new old stock", kits with wrong values or voltage
      ratings, and dangerous substitutions, such as a polarized capacitor fitted where a bipolar one belongs. We buy
      from trusted suppliers and test every part before it goes in: capacitance within ±10% and ESR no more than 20%
      above the datasheet value.
    </p>

    <h2>Our parts and practices</h2>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>Low-ESR, 105°C electrolytics from Nichicon, Panasonic, Elna and Vishay</li>
      <li>Film capacitors for coupling positions</li>
      <li>Polarity verified against the schematic, because silkscreens can be wrong</li>
      <li>First power-up on a dim-bulb tester or variac</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When a repair isn't worth it</h2>
    <p>
      If a unit is worth less than about $200 and needs more than ten capacitors, the repair usually isn't economical.
      We'll tell you honestly, and suggest whether it's better as a parts donor or sold as-is.
    </p>

    <p class="notice">Our rule is simple: do no harm to the patient. A working original board is worth more than a damaged "recapped" one.</p>

    <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href={site.portal.newTicket}>Start a service request</a></p>
  </div></section>
</Base>
